Problem Statement



Write a program to validate an email address and display suitable exceptions if there is any mistake.



Create three custom exception classes:

DotException
AtTheRateException
DomainException


An email address is considered valid only if:

It contains exactly one @
It contains exactly one . after @
The email does not start or end with @ or .
The email does not end with .
The domain extension is the part after the last dot
The domain extension must be one of in, com, net, biz
If any condition fails, throw the respective exception and print the exception message followed by:

Invalid email address



If all conditions are satisfied, print:

Valid email address



Note: You may use predefined string functions such as endsWith, indexOf, lastIndexOf, substring, and charAt.

Input format :
The first line of input contains the email address to be validated.

Output format :
Print one of the following messages exactly:



DotException: Invalid Dot usage

Invalid email address



AtTheRateException: Invalid @ usage

Invalid email address



DomainException: Invalid Domain

Invalid email address



Valid email address



Refer to the sample input and output for format specifications.

Code constraints :
5 ≤ length(email) ≤ 50

Email contains only alphanumeric characters, ., and @

Exactly one @ must be present

Exactly one . must appear after @

Email must not start or end with @ or .

Domain extension = text after the last dot

Valid extensions: in, com, net, biz

Sample test cases :
      Input 1 :
          sample@gmail.com
      Output 1 :
          Valid email address

            
      Input 2 :
          sample@gmail.com.
      Output 2 :
          DotException: Invalid Dot usage
          Invalid email address

            
      Input 3 :
          sample@gmail.con
      Output 3 :
          DomainException: Invalid Domain
          Invalid email address

            
      Input 4 :
          a@b.v
      Output 4 :
          DomainException: Invalid Domain
          Invalid email address
